// Runs the complete native application and verifies the production JusPrin
// shell through Phase 6: installation, Prepare/Slice/Print, the typed
// Agent bridge and tools, persistence, manufacturing history, resize
// and project replacement, fallback, and restoration of stock presentation.
//
// Modes:
//   --dark-ui / --light-ui  process-only appearance for visual checks. macOS
//              sets the Cocoa appearance; Windows writes dark_color_mode into
//              the run's own config, the only input GUI_App::dark_mode reads
//              there (the registry read in check_dark_mode is compiled out).
//   (default)  automated shell checks, exits with the result
//   --stock    automated stock-mode checks (shell disabled via app config),
//              then back into JusPrin through the classic view banner
//   --fresh-example
//              a data directory with no printer runs the first launch:
//              the welcome, "Just slice for now", Back, the add-printer
//              form, then "Skip for now" through to Home, where the
//              agent card signs in by email against a stand-in server
//   --classic-switch
//              switches to classic view and back twice, once through the View
//              menu row and once through the banner
//   --classic-switch-capture <output-directory>
//              the same run, capturing the window in classic view
//   --manual   installs nothing extra and leaves the app open for a human
//   --manual-live-agent
//              leaves the isolated JusPrin shell open with the OpenAI
//              provider enabled for hands-on testing
//   --manual-unconfigured
//              leaves the isolated JusPrin shell open with no Agent service
//              configured at all -- the dock's not-set-up empty state
//   --file-corpus <manifest.json> <output.json> [--file-corpus-config <config.json>]
//              loads real files through the native UI path and records each
//              file-load report beside the selected setup before and after
//   --project-prompt-requests <script.json> <output.json>
//              plays tests/project_prompt's scripted conversations through the
//              project chat's real adapter, tools and host, with the model's
//              outputs scripted and nothing sent over the network, and writes
//              the request bodies the script marks, for that suite to send
//   --plan-journey <model-file> <exchange-directory>
//              loads the model into a clean project with a live provider
//              Agent (JUSPRIN_JOURNEY_PROVIDER openai or anthropic, its
//              OPENAI_API_KEY or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, JUSPRIN_JOURNEY_MODEL) and
//              plays one chat through files: after each turn settles it writes
//              turn-N.json and waits for next-N.txt, the next message or END
//   --request-log <directory>
//              with --live-agent, --manual-live-agent, --plan-journey or
//              --project-prompt-requests: writes every request the project
//              chat's Agent posts to the directory, byte for byte, with the
//              response's usage beside it (tests/jusprin_support/RequestLog.hpp;
//              tests/project_prompt/prefix_check.py compares them)
//   --printer-menu
//              checks the saved-printer rows and selection in the header menu
//   --printer-menu-capture <output-directory>
//              runs the same checks and captures the open menu
//   --task-chat
//              exercises printer and filament help from Prepare, Back, each
//              manual settings escape, and saved-chat isolation
//   --live-agent
//              uses OPENAI_API_KEY and verifies live context/attachment use,
//              reload recovery, native mutation, and the model follow-ups
//   --mcp     real TCP discovery/read and mutation using the shared runtime
//   --mcp-bridge  same native scenario through a persistent stdio subprocess
//   --mcp-setup   native setup-command lifetime, output and argument checks;
//                 uses this harness as a fixture, never edits client config
//   --dialog-routing <output-directory>
//                 makes OrcaSlicer raise its own dialogs through agent tools
//                 and checks none reaches the screen over the chat
//                 (dialog_routing_scenario.inc); pictures any that does
//   --dialog-witness <output-directory>
//                 with any mode: says on stderr which of OrcaSlicer's dialogs
//                 reached the screen (HARNESS DIALOG SHOWN), pictures each and
//                 closes it with the answer that changes least
//   --manual-mcp <fixture-directory>
//                 leaves a two-plate native fixture open for real MCP clients;
//                 exposes Orca's sidebar for native-edit/stale-revision checks;
//                 reuse the directory to test restarts without reconfiguration;
//                 with --fake-printer, the project's printer is first a
//                 saved Bambu A1 mini connected to the in-process fake
//                 (src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md)
//   --header-visual <fixture-directory>
//                 same fixture without the expert sidebar, with the header menu open
//   --right-pane-visual <fixture-directory>
//                 production-like right-pane fixture: expert sidebar hidden,
//                 Agent open, no project-header menu covering the layout
//   --live-agent-unavailable
//              selects OpenAI with consent withheld and verifies that the
//              application stays usable without silently selecting the mock;
//              the dock shows the offer over the empty chat and, once the
//              fixture's edit makes a thread, the setup bar under it, which
//              opens Preferences on its Agent section; the pane draws no
//              setup of its own
//   --slice-all-cold
//              guards Slice all on a multi-plate project before the Preview
//              canvas has ever rendered — the conditions of upstream crash
//              #15116 (null all-plates stats item; fixed upstream by
//              83723e2a7b). Observed 2026-08-31: it PASSES on this tree
//              because the startup color-mode sync also runs
//              _init_select_plate_toolbar on the Preview canvas before any
//              render. Kept as a guard in case that incidental init path
//              changes; run it alongside the default and --stock modes.
//              The synchronous Prepare-only slice command made this reachable
//              on 2026-09-04. The no-auto-preview policy now also leaves the
//              unopened Preview's plate selection unchanged.
//   --recomputing-capture <output-directory>
//              slices the fixture, scales the cube to 120 mm and re-slices,
//              asserts the setup card says the estimates are recomputing,
//              and shows no earlier figure, while the slice runs, and writes
//              recomputing-agent-pane.png and
//              recomputing-shell.png to the directory (handoff item 6)
//   --setup-differences-capture <output-directory>
//              saves the fixture as a 3MF and opens it as a person does,
//              asserting the setup card holds only the presets and the
//              estimates and the chat says "Opened card-opened.3mf as a new
//              project" (setup-differences-opened). It then edits process,
//              filament and per-object settings the way a person does,
//              asserts the setup card lists none of them but counts them,
//              the object override included, and the setup page names every
//              one; writes setup-differences-card.png,
//              setup-differences-page.png and setup-differences-page-end.png
//              to the directory. It then adds a support blocker to the cube,
//              opens the cube's own view and presses "Use project value"
//              under Wall loops and "Remove" under the blocker, asserting
//              each row goes, each is one named undo step logged as the
//              person's, and Orca's undo brings each back
//              (setup-differences-object, setup-differences-object-after);
//              then puts the values back and asserts the count goes. It then
//              records a purpose, pins a plan and applies a patch through the
//              agent's own tools, slices, and writes the card: purpose,
//              headline, presets and estimates, with no moment of change
//              (setup-differences-agent-card), and its page
//              (setup-differences-agent-page); the plan stays current
//              through the agent's own patch. A hand edit then annotates the
//              plan line "Sparse infill density changed to … by hand" with a
//              Check plan link (setup-differences-edited-card) and marks the plan
//              needs_reassessment; a setup_record with only the headline
//              clears it and keeps the purpose; an imported part marks it
//              again with the import's name (setup-differences-imported-card).
//   --calibration-capture <output-directory>
//              the three Calibrate… rows with a stand-in project assistant:
//              the filament row's and the Home card's dialogs are cancelled
//              and change nothing; the printer row's is accepted, the
//              assistant asks which test, the Temperature chip is tapped,
//              and calibration_generate opens the test's own project and
//              chat. Writes a picture of the window, menu or dialog at each
//              step
//   --reply-choices-capture <output-directory>
//              a stand-in project assistant ends its reply with a Choices
//              line; asserts the page draws it as chips and not as text,
//              taps one, and asserts it arrives as the person's message and
//              the chips go once answered. Writes reply-choices-offered.png
//              and reply-choices-answered.png (the Agent pane) and
//              reply-choices-window.png (the window, from the screen)
//   --timeline-capture <output-directory>
//              records a build, mirrors the object three times and edits a
//              print setting by hand, asserts the thread's change rows, and writes
//              timeline-agent-pane-<light|dark>.png (revision-timeline B10)
//   --figma-timeline-capture <output-directory>
//              shows the entire First print story from the two Figma timeline
//              frames in the real Agent WebView, then captures it with the
//              revert confirmation closed and open. Uses deterministic records
//              and an isolated project; no OpenAI key or printer is needed.
//   --figma-timeline-manual
//              leaves that isolated timeline open for hands-on inspection.
//   --external-project-history [--external-project-history-capture <output-directory>]
//              checks the repeated external 3MF choice and explicit,
//              confirmed version restore; optionally captures a history row
//   --reopen-prompt <output-directory>
//              shows the question asked when a file opened before is opened
//              again, in its four variants (the open project or an earlier
//              one; the file unchanged or changed), draws each to a PNG and
//              answers it Cancel
//   --file-opening
//              every way a file is opened (architecture.md, "Opening and
//              importing files"): opened from the OS, Home's Open… with its
//              dialog stood in for, dropped on Home and on the Prepare canvas,
//              a .3mf on a full plate, and the canvas drop after a slice
//   --chat-restoration
//              checks historical viewing and explicit chat/model/planning
//              restoration in an isolated managed project
//   --printer-live
//              needs OPENAI_API_KEY: the printer-agent-tools handoff's worked
//              examples through the real printer panel and the live model --
//              words and taps sent as the page sends them, every exchange
//              printed as HARNESS LIVE lines, and the mechanical outcomes
//              checked (cards drawn or not, the settings change, the saved
//              nozzle, Undo). The key is written to this run's throwaway
//              app config, where the panel reads it.
//   --printer-settings-live
//              needs OPENAI_API_KEY: the header's Ask AI about printer settings… on the
//              printer OrcaSlicer ships, as the fixture selects it, through
//              the live model: a retraction change is saved as
//              "<preset> - Copy"; the copy holds it, is
//              selected, and is what the conversation is about
//   --filament-settings-live
//              needs OPENAI_API_KEY: the header's Ask AI about filament settings… for slot
//              1, which holds a filament OrcaSlicer ships, through the live
//              model: a nozzle temperature change is saved as
//              "<preset> - Copy"; the copy holds it and
//              takes the slot
//   --printer-live-no-plugin
//              needs OPENAI_API_KEY and a machine without the Bambu network
//              plug-in: adds a Bambu Lab printer through the live model and
//              asks to connect it, from the add flow and from Home's
//              Connect…, with the fake printer off. Checks what the app
//              shows: the opening names the plug-in, not LAN mode; its
//              notice is drawn once; no credential form opens; a connect the
//              model tries anyway is refused for the plug-in; and Done ends
//              it. The model's own words are printed for reading
//   --printer-connect-capture <output-directory>
//              needs OPENAI_API_KEY: connects a Klipper printer
//              through the real printer panel and the live model, against
//              print hosts on loopback ports, and writes a PNG of the panel
//              with the local credential form open and of Home after.
//              The pictures come from the embedded browser's own snapshot.
//   --manual-tool-strip
//              leaves the shell open on the two-plate fixture with an object
//              selected, for hands-on testing of the canvas tool strip
//   --print-issues
//              the print issues on the Prepare canvas, through their whole
//              life in the real app: OrcaSlicer's validation, placement,
//              slicing warnings and failures read back as issues; the bubble
//              following camera, zoom, resize and object moves and staying
//              off its object; every way the list and the bubble close;
//              ignoring a warning and its undo; Ask AI through to the chat;
//              with no agent, the setup button through Preferences › Agent
//              and back to Ask AI; stale and cleared issues; deletion, undo, plate switch, project
//              replacement; OrcaSlicer's own notices not drawn, in Prepare
//              or in Check print
//   --print-issues-capture <output-directory>
//              the same run, saving the canvas's own pixels at each stage
//   --check-print / --check-print-capture <output-directory>
//              the Check print window on three sliced plates (nothing to
//              flag, support generated, a slab printed in the air): the
//              report panel's contents, the Legend switch, a selected
//              finding moving the view, the brand accent on the sliders,
//              and the window closing itself when its slice goes stale; the
//              capture form also saves the panel and the window's canvas
//   --print-issue-readers
//              the conditions that need another printer or a slice: two
//              nozzles, mixed materials, paths outside the plate, a failed
//              slice naming its object, and a result confirmed on the canvas
//   --manual-print-issues / --manual-print-issues-no-agent
//              leaves two overlapping cubes open for hands-on use, with a
//              stand-in assistant that answers "Noted.", or with none
//   --tool-strip-capture <output-directory>
//              clicks the Prepare canvas's tool strip with real pointer
//              events: toggles Move and Scale, follows the Rotate shortcut,
//              duplicates and undoes, opens More (Windows), orbits and
//              narrows the window; asserts the open tool, selection and
//              instance count at each step, and writes tool-strip-*.png
//   --home-live
//              saves and connects the in-process fake Bambu printer, then
//              drives it Online, Printing and Offline while Home stays on
//              screen, reading the printer card from the Home page itself:
//              every change must arrive without leaving Home, a card menu
//              opened first must stay open, and nothing is sent while the
//              printer is unchanged or Home is hidden. About a minute; the
//              Offline step waits out the 30-second freshness window
//   --printer-setup
//              drives the Add a printer modal from the printer menu with the
//              deterministic recognizer: dismissal and scrim lifetime, the
//              choice/Not this one/Start over/correction transitions, adding a
//              shipped but disabled profile, the network state, Set it up
//              myself, and install rollback; the chat's no-agent offer
//              through Preferences › Agent and back to the same session;
//              then named printers: a second printer of one model, Home's
//              list, rename, remove, and a nozzle change that keeps the
//              printer's own settings
//   --printer-network-check <fixture-directory> <address> <plug-in-version> [--simulate-announce]
//              no model and no fake: against a real Bambu Lab P1S in cloud
//              mode at <address>, on this network, with Bambu's real network
//              plug-in copied to <fixture-directory>/plugins beforehand. Saves
//              a P1S, opens its Connect chat and runs printer_bambu_connect and
//              printer_connection_status with a scripted stand-in for the
//              model, printing each result and each note the app posts. Sends
//              nothing to the printer: it only listens for its announcements
//              and is refused before any credential is asked for. With
//              --simulate-announce, no printer is needed: 25 seconds in, the
//              app is handed the announcement the plug-in would have passed on
//   --printer-connect-skill <fixture-directory> <address> <plug-in-version>
//              no model and no fake, like --printer-network-check, whatever mode
//              the printer at <address> is in: what skill reaches the model, and
//              when. A chat that only adds a printer gets none; a chat about the
//              printer gets it with its first connection tool's result; a chat
//              opened to connect has it on its first request. Prints each state
//              the app reports and the skill's line for it, then stops at the
//              app's access code request at the latest. <plug-in-version> "none"
//              is the person with no plug-in: leave plugins/ out of the fixture
//              directory and the printer need not be there.
//   --printer-live-p1s <address> [lan | wrong-code | cloud-on | real-code | no-printer]
//              needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or OPENAI_API_KEY, macOS, and Bambu's real
//              network plug-in in ~/Library/Application Support/JusPrin2/plugins,
//              which it copies to this run's throwaway data directory: against
//              a real Bambu Lab P1S in cloud mode at <address>, saves a P1S, opens
//              its Connect chat and plays a person through the live model: asks
//              to connect it, gives the address, and at least 20 seconds into the
//              search asks what to do. Prints every message, tool call and note
//              with its timing, and checks the status was read, no connect
//              succeeded, and what the model then said. Costs one live chat each
//              run; other programs on UDP 2021 hide the printer (see Testing/README.md)
//              A scenario plays the connection skill's chat against a printer in
//              LAN mode instead: lan stops at the access code form; wrong-code
//              types a made-up code twice, and answers the report card; cloud-on
//              starts in cloud mode and waits, up to 15 minutes, for someone to
//              turn LAN Only on; real-code waits as long for someone to type the
//              real code into the form, or takes it from JUSPRIN_P1S_CODE, and
//              never prints it.
//   --printer-live-host <script>
//              needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or OPENAI_API_KEY: plays a person
//              connecting a print host through the live model, from a script
//              (see queue_printer_live_host). Prints every message, tool call
//              and note with its timing. Costs one live chat each run.
//   --print-host-check <cases-file> [<catalogue id>]
//              no model: a printer chat's connection tool called once per
//              line of "<moonraker|octoprint> <address> [<variable holding the
//              key> | MISSING | CANCEL]", printing what the model reads back.
//              When the host asks for a key the call waits on the form, which
//              is submitted with that variable's value (or empty), or answered
//              with its "no key" or Cancel button. For the printer named
//              (such as "Elegoo/Elegoo Neptune 4 Pro") or the first one not
//              made by Bambu Lab.
//   --account-box
//              the server's box on Preferences › Account in the real web
//              view: HTML whose scripts would close the window through the
//              app's bridge is measured and shown, runs none of them, and
//              leaves the bridge working
//   --server-sheet [capture-dir]
//              needs JUSPRIN_SERVER_URL and JUSPRIN_TEST_ACCOUNT_TOKEN, a
//              throwaway account's on a test server, which it deletes: Get more
//              credits and Billing and usage open the server's pages in the
//              app's sheet, the done address closes it, Delete account signs
//              out, and signed out the sheet opens Credits and plans as a guest
//   --server-sheet-popup [capture-dir]
//              a pop-up the sheet's page opens, as the checkout opens PayPal's
//              window, opens in the app's own window with window.opener: it
//              reports back to the page and closes, against a stand-in server
//   --printer-chat-sheet [capture-dir]
//              the printer chat's Get more credits and Billing and usage open
//              the server's pages in the app's sheet, and its Get the new
//              version… opens Preferences on Updates; with JUSPRIN_SERVER_URL
//              the sheet loads that server's pages, signed out
//   --print-history [capture-dir]
//              the G-code record end to end with the in-process fake Bambu
//              printer: an export through the export_file path, two sends
//              through OrcaSlicer's own Select Machine dialog, the first
//              print started and finished through the fake's control file,
//              the second failed (nothing written), and the record in the
//              saved project document; then the Print history screen opened
//              from the project pane and from Home, Back, Export… from the
//              kept copy, a hand-edited export read as changed and Export
//              again beside it, and Open in chat at the marker; then Print
//              again, checked to upload the kept package the first send left,
//              byte for byte, rather than an export of the plate

